// §6.6.1, an "It shall be an error" bullet, verbatim: "It shall be an error if the
// loop generate scheme does not terminate." The scheme is evaluated during
// elaboration, "before simulation begins" (§6.6), so a loop that never falsifies
// its condition is not a hang at run time — it is a model that cannot be
// elaborated at all, and the LRM requires the diagnostic.
//
// `i` starts at 0 and the step is (i + 1) % 3, so `i` runs 0, 1, 2, 0, ... and
// `i < 3` is true forever. VerA reports it at the rule: E0420, naming the
// genvar value that repeats. It follows the scheme alone until it ends or
// repeats a value, with no iteration count standing in for the answer
// (genvar_loop_4097_iterations.va, the legal neighbour, ends past the 4096
// iterations VerA once stopped at).
//
// Until 2026-10-15 this file's step was `i = i - 1`. A genvar is a §3.2
// integer, whose arithmetic wraps at 32 bits, so that loop runs 0, -1, ...,
// -2^31, wraps to 2^31 - 1 and ends: 2^31 + 1 iterations, legal, and unrolled
// in full now that no cap refuses it. The claim moved to the E0420 explain
// text (`vera --explain E0420`) and Vague_Decisions.md §7.
//
// The block body is an EMPTY `begin end`, and that is deliberate. Syntax 6-8 reads
// `generate_block ::= module_or_generate_item | begin [ : id ] { module_or_generate_item }
// end`, and a contribution statement is neither — it is reachable only through
// `analog_construct ::= analog analog_statement`. This file used to write a bare
// `I(p) <+ V(p);` there, which pinned a VerA parser extension no conforming compiler
// implements: a conforming compiler rejects such a source at the block body and
// never evaluates the loop scheme, so the E0420 below would have been proved by
// nothing. An empty begin-end is a legal generate block and adds no second rule for
// the diagnostic to fire from.
//
// The neighbouring bullet, "It shall be an error if a genvar value is repeated
// during the evaluation of the loop generate scheme", has no separate fixture on
// purpose: the genvar is the loop's entire state and the step is a function of it
// alone, so a repeated value is a cycle, and a cycle in a condition that already
// passed once means the loop never terminates. Every repeated-value source is a
// non-terminating source, and this file is both.
//
//! lrm 6.6.1
//! lrm 6.6.1:7
//! lrm 6.6.1:6
//! reject-only E0420
//! reject-only `i` repeats the value 0
//! neighbour genvar_loop_4097_iterations.va
module ch6_generate_nonterminating(p);
  inout p; electrical p;
  genvar i;
  generate
    for (i = 0; i < 3; i = (i + 1) % 3) begin end
  endgenerate
endmodule
